    <script type="application/ld+json">
      {"@context":"http://schema.org","@graph":[{"@context":"http://schema.org","@type":"SocialMediaPosting","author":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EFAMA","url":"https://be.linkedin.com/company/efama"},"datePublished":"2026-10-12T09:21:21.237Z","interactionStatistic":{"@type":"InteractionCounter","interactionType":"http://schema.org/LikeAction","userInteractionCount":0},"mainEntityOfPage":"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/efama_assetmanagement-capitalmarkets-consolidatedtape-activity-7515340826164510722-RblT","text":"📣 Last week, EFAMA’s Thomas Tilley and Susan Yavari participated in the Bulgarian Association of Asset Management Companies (Българска асоциация на управляващите дружества) conference \"NextGen vol.02: New Horizons 2026\" in Sofia, Bulgaria.\n\n🎤 Thomas presented on key trends in the European and Bulgarian fund markets. Some of the key insights include:\n\n🔸 Fund costs are coming down across all main types of long-term UCITS.\n🔸 The European fund industry is becoming increasingly international.\n🔸 Passive and larger funds are swiftly growing in market share\n🔸 Bulgaria is a small fund market, however, its relative net asset growth in recent years was significantly stronger than in Europe as a whole.\n\n🎤 Susan joined a panel discussion moderated by Ilina Biserova (Bulgarian Stock Exchange) featuring Assoc. Prof. Manyu Moravenov Ph.D MRICS, Ph.D. (Bulgarian Stock Exchange), Eglantine Desautel (EuroCTP) and Ludovic Aigrot (Nasdaq) on the Consolidated Tape. The discussion highlighted several important points:\n \n🔹 Most panelists agreed that introduction of a consolidated tape should not be viewed solely through the prism of market data revenues.\n🔹 Exchanges’ data revenues were not at risk as the majority of subscribers will continue to consume the lower latency exchange feeds, in addition to the CT. \n🔹 A more transparent and accessible European equity market is the true prize that the CT delivers. This will benefit investment inflows, liquidity and ultimately IPO activity also.\n🔹 ETFs continue to be the fastest growing segment in the fund sector. Additional transparency ( both on pre and post-trade data) supports in-flows into European ETFs, and helps ETF issuers include a broader range of shares, including small and mid-cap, as well as Central and East European stocks.\n\n🙏 Many thanks to the panelists for an interesting discussion and to the Bulgarian Association of Asset Management Companies for the invitation. Congratulations to Natalia Petrova on all the preparation underway for the October 2027 T+1 migration.\n\nJim Goldie Giovanni Sabatini Jacopo Borgognone Corentin Couvidat Rajat Tawari\n\n#AssetManagement #CapitalMarkets #ConsolidatedTape","url":"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/efama_assetmanagement-capitalmarkets-consolidatedtape-activity-7515340826164510722-RblT"},{"@context":"http://schema.org","@type":"SocialMediaPosting","author":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EFAMA","url":"https://be.linkedin.com/company/efama"},"datePublished":"2026-10-12T06:31:13.899Z","interactionStatistic":{"@type":"InteractionCounter","interactionType":"http://schema.org/LikeAction","userInteractionCount":3},"mainEntityOfPage":"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/efama_assetmanagement-capitalmarkets-households-activity-7515298013486039040-mmy_","text":"💶 Who is saving for pension in Europe?\n\nAs shown in the third edition of the Household Report, published last July, the extent to which households save for retirement is one of the most important factors helping to explain their participation in capital markets. The high level of pension fund savings in several European countries reflects long-lasting policy efforts to achieve high coverage of funded pensions through mandatory or quasi-mandatory arrangements.\n\n📊 The visual below highlights how five European countries — the Netherlands, Sweden, the United Kingdom, Norway and Denmark — stand out significantly from the rest of Europe in terms of pension fund savings per capita.\n\n🔗 Check the full report here: https://lnkd.in/exu7ReWd \n\n#AssetManagement #CapitalMarkets #Households #Pensions","url":"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/efama_assetmanagement-capitalmarkets-households-activity-7515298013486039040-mmy_"},{"@context":"http://schema.org","@type":"SocialMediaPosting","author":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EFAMA","url":"https://be.linkedin.com/company/efama"},"datePublished":"2026-10-09T09:21:39.149Z","headline":"","interactionStatistic":{"@type":"InteractionCounter","interactionType":"http://schema.org/LikeAction","userInteractionCount":20},"mainEntityOfPage":"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/efama_assetmanagement-worldinvestorweek-wiw2026-activity-7514253737692135426-UG5o","text":"🎓 As we celebrate the 10th edition of the International Organization of Securities Commissions - IOSCO’s World Investor Week, we are proud to look back on EFAMA’s long-standing commitment to investor education and financial literacy, helping make investment information more accessible, understandable and relevant for investors.\n\nOver the years, we have:\n 📚 Developed 3 investor education brochures\n 🌍 Translated our materials into 20 languages\n 🎤 Organised 13 webinars and events\n\n🙏 A big thank you to EFAMA’s Investor Education Platform and our members for their continued engagement, valuable contributions and support in advancing investor education and financial literacy across Europe.\n\nAs we look ahead, we are ready to build on this work and continue our commitment for the next 10 years!\n\n🔗 Check out our work on Investor Education: https://lnkd.in/ditMY76 \n\n#AssetManagement #WorldInvestorWeek #WIW2026 #InvestorEducation #FinancialLiteracy","url":"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/efama_assetmanagement-worldinvestorweek-wiw2026-activity-7514253737692135426-UG5o"},{"@context":"http://schema.org","@type":"SocialMediaPosting","author":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EFAMA","url":"https://be.linkedin.com/company/efama"},"datePublished":"2026-10-09T07:21:00.158Z","interactionStatistic":{"@type":"InteractionCounter","interactionType":"http://schema.org/LikeAction","userInteractionCount":17},"mainEntityOfPage":"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/efama_assetmanagement-misp-integratedreporting-activity-7514223375171588097-3oez","text":"📍 Last week, members of EFAMA’s Management Companies Regulation & Services Standing Committee met in Brussels to discuss some key topics, including the ongoing MISP negotiations.\n\n Key takeaways included:\n \n🔹Changes to the supervision of large asset management groups, if any, should concentrate on convergence, not transfering powers away from national supervisors or empowering ESMA to over-rule their decisions. \n\n🔹The creation of an integrated fund reporting system in the near future by ESMA, would be beneficial to the work of any potential ‘convergence colleges’.\n\n🔹The ongoing work on integrated fund reporting has to stay faithful to the reform principles of (i) simplification and reduction of burden associated with reporting, (ii) avoiding double-reporting of the same data, (iii) making use of data-sharing arrangements, and (iv) choosing the best solution in terms of cost-benefit analysis.\n \n🔹The notion of letter-box entities has already been clearly defined for the asset management industry, with sufficient powers conveyed to competent authorities to assess delegation structures. Reopening this discussion and asking ESMA to define this in an RTS will not further the MISP’s goals. \n\n🔹The unfair treatment of investment funds as “unrated corporates” under the rules of CRD/CRR requires a solution to avoid negative implications for securities lending activities. \n \n🙏 A warm thank you to all members of the Standing Committee for taking the time to see us in Brussels. These exchanges are the backbone of our everyday work and an excellent opportunity to bring the industry together on those important topics.\n \n👉 Find out more about our work on management companies here: https://lnkd.in/eSftFbuV \n\nStephane Aidan, FRM, Francois Baratte, Zuzanna Bogusz, Santo Borsellino Laetitia Boucquey Geraldine Brehony Louise Campbell Maria Canali De Rossi Federico Cupelli Rachel Ellison Piotr Giemza-Popowski Stéphane Janin Thomas Ketter Wouter Kuijpers Philippa List Tiarnach Mallon Barbara Marin Klajnšek Rayhan Oddud Andreea Pencea, CFA Dee Ruddy Peggy Henrich Silke Wagner-Kautz Wall Helene Evelyne Christiaens Taha Bousmaha\n\n#AssetManagement #MISP #IntegratedReporting","url":"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/efama_assetmanagement-misp-integratedreporting-activity-7514223375171588097-3oez"},{"@context":"http://schema.org","@type":"SocialMediaPosting","author":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EFAMA","url":"https://be.linkedin.com/company/efama"},"datePublished":"2026-10-08T17:43:57.650Z","interactionStatistic":{"@type":"InteractionCounter","interactionType":"http://schema.org/LikeAction","userInteractionCount":19},"mainEntityOfPage":"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/efama_fund-retailinvestment-pension-activity-7514017759865794562-P1OV","text":"Earlier this week, EFAMA Director General Tanguy van de WERVE spoke at the 2026 conference of The International Investment Funds Association (IIFA), superbly organised by The Securities and Investment Management Association (SIMA) in the beautiful city of Toronto 🇨🇦.\r\n\nSpeaking about ways to increase retirement savings and retail participation in capital markets, he highlighted a number of lessons from different European markets, including:\r\n\n- What has proven to work\r\n\n1/ The power of default\r\n\nDefaults can help address inertia and overcome the behavioural tendency to postpone investing. Properly designed auto-enrolment in workplace pension schemes, with the possibility to opt out, can be highly effective, as the UK experience demonstrates.\r\n\n2/ Simple tax wrappers\r\n\nThe success of Sweden’s Investment Savings Account (ISK) suggests that a winning formula combines a simple tax wrapper with digital distribution, simple products, low costs and a population that has become familiar with capital markets over many years.\r\n\n3/ Making regular investing effortless\r\n\nInvestment plans with digital onboarding that enable people to invest small amounts regularly and automatically are proving increasingly successful in a number of EU countries.\r\n\n- What does not work\r\n\n1/ Too long and complex disclosures\r\n\nTransparency is very important, but information overload must be avoided. Excessive disclosure can overwhelm investors rather than help them. The objective should be to enable informed decision-making, not to scare people away from investing. Smoking kills, investing does not.\r\n\n2/ Overly stringent investor protection requirements\r\n\nInvestor protection is essential, but poorly designed or disproportionate requirements can inhibit both supply and demand. ELTIF 1.0 and PEPP 1.0 are notable examples.\r\n\n3/ Poorly designed tax incentives\r\n\nTax incentives need to be meaningful enough to change behaviour. At the same time, if they primarily benefit higher earners who would have invested anyway, their effectiveness as a use of public money should be questioned.\r\n\r\n\nThe ensuing discussion with his fellow panelists from Oceania, Africa and Latin America reinforced a clear message: across markets, simplicity is essential to encouraging greater retail participation in capital markets.\r\n\nFrom that perspective, it is highly regrettable that the Retail Investment Strategy (RIS) which will be put to a plenary vote in the European Parliament in November will add costs and complexity without sufficiently demonstrated benefits for retail investors. \n\nThis is all the more puzzling given the broad consensus that the current text of the RIS will not achieve its objectives, while the resulting additional complexity runs directly counter to the EU’s stated commitment to simplification and competitiveness.\n\n#fund #retailinvestment #pension #retirementsavings #transparency #SIU #RIS","url":"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/efama_fund-retailinvestment-pension-activity-7514017759865794562-P1OV"},{"@context":"http://schema.org","@type":"SocialMediaPosting","author":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EFAMA","url":"https://be.linkedin.com/company/efama"},"datePublished":"2026-10-08T09:30:17.531Z","interactionStatistic":{"@type":"InteractionCounter","interactionType":"http://schema.org/LikeAction","userInteractionCount":6},"mainEntityOfPage":"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/efama_ucits-fundpassport-singlemarket-activity-7513893524082388993-HqLM","text":"🛠️ In part 4 of our LinkedIn series discussing MISP and cross-border UCITS, we assess the parts of the proposal that could benefit from further improvement. The current proposal already does a good job at improving cross-border distribution of UCITS. However, if EU competitiveness and regulatory simplification are the goals, there is more we can do to remove complexity and avoid new rules that are unclear in practice. Now is the perfect opportunity.\n\nThis includes:\n🔹 Simple, standardised fund authorisation documents that avoid duplications.\n🔹 Keeping compliance checks on marketing materials AFTER authorisation.\n🔹 Some flexibility in harmonised marketing requirements.\n🔹 Clarifying what a ‘material’ change to a fund is, while also simplifying notification of these.\n🔹 No duplicative supervisory fees for the same procedure or service.\n🔹 No Member State requirements to maintain a physical presence in the ‘host’ jurisdiction.\n\n📑 One thing MISP does not address is various national reporting requirements imposed by some Member States. This often adds a huge amount of unnecessary paperwork for fund managers. However, ESMA’s plans for an integrated fund reporting system could eliminate these, which would greatly reduce complexity. This is where real improvements in the supervision of EU asset management can be made, not a single EU supervisor or annual reviews of large cross-border entities by ESMA.\n\nWe will have more about this soon!\n\n👉 You can find our full position on the MISP here: https://lnkd.in/eTRBhsDU\n\n#UCITS #fundpassport #SingleMarket #MISP","url":"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/efama_ucits-fundpassport-singlemarket-activity-7513893524082388993-HqLM"},{"@context":"http://schema.org","@type":"SocialMediaPosting","author":{"@type":"Organization","name":"EFAMA","url":"https://be.linkedin.com/company/efama"},"datePublished":"2026-10-08T08:21:41.150Z","interactionStatistic":{"@type":"InteractionCounter","interactionType":"http://schema.org/LikeAction","userInteractionCount":21},"mainEntityOfPage":"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/efama_securitisation-siu-eueconomy-activity-7513876258729025537--uvj","text":"📝 Today, EFAMA and AFME (Association for Financial Markets in Europe) have released a joint statement calling on policymakers to avoid including duplicative and disproportionate penalties in the new EU securitisation regime. As trilogue negotiations near their conclusion, it is important that the final agreement delivers on its core objective: attracting investment and channelling capital to Europe's economy.\n \n👉 Read the full statement here: https://lnkd.in/eNRZfnuR\n \nA workable solution for the industry should encourage investor participation and support market growth, allowing securitisation to play a greater role in financing businesses, households and the transition towards a more competitive European economy. \n\nToo many times in the past we have gotten this wrong. Let's get it right this time.\n \n#securitisation #SIU #EUEconomy #capitalmarkets","url":"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/efama_securitisation-siu-eueconomy-activity-7513876258729025537--uvj"},{"@context":"http://schema.org","@type":"Organization","name":"EFAMA","url":"https://be.linkedin.com/company/efama","address":{"type":"PostalAddress","streetAddress":"47 Rue Montoyer ","addressLocality":"Brussels","addressRegion":"Belgium","postalCode":"1000 ","addressCountry":"BE"},"description":"EFAMA is the voice of the European investment management industry.  Through our 28 member associations, 59 corporate members and 25 associate members, we represent all the diversity and richness of the European industry. \n\nOur corporate members are global and regional asset management companies with significant intra-EU operations. \n\nWe strongly support the EU project, and have done so since our establishment in 1974. \n\nEFAMA is a recognised source of relevant market data. \n\nWe act in full transparency and are always clear as regards who we represent.\n\nWe are in Europe and for Europe. And have always been.","numberOfEmployees":{"value":69,"@type":"QuantitativeValue"},"logo":{"contentUrl":"https://media.licdn.com/dms/image/v2/D4E0BAQEuY_GLAa54zA/company-logo_200_200/B4EZapp_zaHYAM-/0/1746603081290/efama_logo?e=2147483647&v=beta&t=3B9hgDbJRKOxWcilhuI7YoIOrkzWDFFqML-3mP5-NWk","description":"EFAMA","@type":"ImageObject"},"slogan":"The voice of the investment management industry | From Europe, for Europe","sameAs":"http://www.efama.org"}]}
    </script>
  
  
      
